</ul><h1>A Knack For Snacks</h1><p>BeanVIVO. Is it:</p>
<p>A. Something your uncle just said to the waiter at a Mexican restaurant, trying (and failing) to call upon a Spanish class he took his senior year in high school (which was more than three decades ago), and embarrassing your entire family in the process?</p>
<p>B. A seasoning packet sold in the 80s and 90s posing as a mid-week meal solution: “just add beans and ground turkey for a healthy, scrumptious dinner!”?</p>
<p>Or…</p>
<p>C. A hearty, protein-rich, fiber-packed snack mix?</p>
<p>If you selected C, you’re correct!</p>
<p>Although, to be fair, if you selected A, you also might be correct. In which case, we’re really sorry.</p>
<p>Anyway, let’s back this up with some stats, in terms of their protein and fiber content. You get 7 grams of protein per serving, which isn’t crazy, but it’s probably about the max you can do for a snack that’s not just a chicken breast or a weird mealy protein bar. Beyond that, you get a nice 5 grams of fiber.</p>
<p>Which Amazon customer Laura <a href="https://www.amazon.com/review/R291BYCII25CWG/ref=cm_cr_dp_d_rvw_ttl?ie=UTF8&tag=mehdown-20">certainly appreciates</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These are so good! I’ve been trying to increase my fiber via food instead of relying on supplements, but most snacks boasting high fiber have a terrible texture. These actually had a great crunch to them and the flavor wasn’t too strong. Definitely like the pickle flavor the most, with the sea salt as a close second. I’m a wimp with heat so I’m not sure if I’d buy the Habanero BBQ again. Bonus points for the resealable bag - makes for an easy snack to toss in my desk drawer!</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/review/RYM8UPPJ9IX49/ref=cm_cr_dp_d_rvw_ttl?ie=UTF8&tag=mehdown-20">The heat was an issue for Amazon customer HAS, as well</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Spicier than I would prefer as a snack. Perhaps sprinkled in a salad or something I would find it more palatable.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The salad topper idea is a good one. After all, a pop of heat in a refreshing salad is nice, but sometimes you don’t want bite after bite of spice.</p>
<p>At any rate, <a href="https://www.amazon.com/review/RLB447STO3PM8/ref=cm_cr_dp_d_rvw_ttl?ie=UTF8&tag=mehdown-20">it didn’t bother Amber</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The three different kinds of this snack was insanely delicious! And the calories were so low I ate as much as I wanted with no guilt. Maybe they were a bit overpriced, but the package size was pleasing, and the quality couldn’t have been better.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Two things to comment on here. First, on the “And the calories were so low I ate as much as I wanted with no guilt” point: while a nut-based snack mix might be healthier than something super-processed, we just should point out that these things boast 150 calories per quarter-cup. Pretty standard for nuts, and not terrible, but it does mean that a bag is about 600 calories.</p>
<p>The other, more pertinent bit, is the price. Over on Amazon, a 12-pack of these would run you about $86. Here on Meh, it’s a cool $18.</p>
<p>Pretty nice, huh? For a pretty good snack, by all accounts?</p>
<p>Or salad topper.</p>
<p>Look, whatever gets you to click the buy button, they’re that, okay?</p>